mx-snapshot (26.11) mx; urgency=medium

  * ISOs made for distribution no longer carry this computer's Tailscale
    connection, snap store registration and logins, root's private files
    such as shell history, keys and saved logins, or (on Arch) its SSH
    host keys.
  * Before making an ISO for distribution, warn when system services hold
    passwords or keys that would be copied into it; MX Snapshot asks
    whether to continue.
  * Only one snapshot can run at a time: starting MX Snapshot or
    iso-snapshot-cli while another snapshot is running now shows a message
    instead of breaking the running one.
  * Stop with an error instead of making an ISO that may not boot when the
    startup files cannot be unpacked or a kernel driver cannot be copied.
  * Work folders whose names contain quotes or dollar signs no longer break
    the snapshot.
  * On Arch, personal backup ISOs show a normal login prompt on the text
    console instead of repeatedly failing to log in a "demo" user.

 -- Adrian <adrian@mxlinux.org>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 11:55:39 -0500

mx-snapshot (26.10.2) mx; urgency=medium

  * Create a SHA-256 checksum file (.sha256) for the ISO instead of SHA-512,
    the kind most distributions publish, which is also faster to make.
  * On Arch, ISOs made for distribution keep the package repositories set up
    on your system, including the MX Arch repository, instead of replacing
    them with the default Arch ones.
  * On Arch, each system started from an ISO made for distribution creates
    its own package signing key on first start, instead of all of them
    sharing the key copied from your system.

 -- Adrian <adrian@mxlinux.org>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 22:31:14 -0400

mx-snapshot (26.10.1) mx; urgency=medium

  * On Arch, fix a security problem in snapshot cleanup, leave the running
    system's timezone and Desktop icons alone, and make sure ISOs built with
    another kernel (such as linux-lts) start correctly.
  * Ctrl+C, stopping the program or logging out now cancels a running
    snapshot cleanly, and the window can no longer freeze, start a second
    snapshot or close before cleanup has finished.
  * Stop with a clear error instead of producing an ISO that does not boot
    when the disk fills up, and fix false "not enough free space" errors.
  * Check the ISO name before starting, so an invalid name is rejected right
    away instead of at the end.
  * Save the right log after each run, in /var/log and, with "Shutdown
    computer when done", next to the ISO.
  * Keep your settings and exclude list working after using the command-line
    tool with sudo.
  * Do not add an unneeded keyboard boot option.

